</span>
              
</span></span></span></p>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11967v1/pro_share_shift_by_evidence_case.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/214;" width="476" height="214"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Average shifts in the “pro” share for each model, by argument configuration. OS = “One-sided”, C&amp;C = “Clear and convincing”.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental setup</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">MillStone</span> benchmark instantiates the methodology of Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Measurement methodology ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> with concrete issues and arguments from the Encyclopedia Britannica’s ProCon website. We then apply <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">MillStone</span> to evaluate open-mindedness of nine LLMs: Gemini 2.0 Flash, Claude 3.5 Haiku and Opus 4, Llama 3.1 7B and 405B, GPT 4o and 4o mini, and Grok 3 and 3 mini. We accessed Gemini, Claude, and Llama models through the Google Cloud Vertex API endpoint, and OpenAI and Grok models through those companies’ respective API endpoints.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Issues dataset</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We constructed our issues dataset <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> by scraping Encyclopedia Britannica’s <a href="https://procon.org" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">ProCon</a> site, which summarizes 107 contentious debates in US society. Each debate is framed by a question and includes cited arguments and quotes in favor and against the framing. Across 107 issues, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> contains 517 “pro” arguments, 498 “con” arguments, and 8,646 citations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This raw corpus cannot be used directly because the debate-framing questions are not neutral, e.g., “Is a vegetarian diet better for us and society?” Using Gemini 2.0 Flash, we rephrased the questions into a neutral form (see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Question rephrasing prompt ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for
our full rephrasing prompt). For example, the above question is rephrased to specify the issue as “vegetarian diets and their effects on individuals and society” with positions “a vegetarian diet is beneficial” and “a vegetarian diet is not beneficial”.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Evaluation harness</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate five models on the full <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">MillStone</span> benchmark: Gemini 2.0 Flash, Claude 3.5 Haiku and Opus 4, and Llama 3.1 7B and 405B. These models have different sizes, cover a range of performance capabilities, and are from different model families. We construct <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\times T\times|C|\times R=107\times 6\times 11\times 15=105,930" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>107</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>105,930</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\times T\times|C|\times R=107\times 6\times 11\times 15=105,930</annotation></semantics></math> evaluative prompts per model,
where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C|=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C|=11</annotation></semantics></math> represents 1 baseline + 2 one-sided + 4 clear and convincing + 4 balanced configurations (varying which side is favored). We evaluate GPT 4o, 4o mini, Grok 3, and 3 mini on a subset of 10 controversial issues where the other models strongly disagree with each other. For this evaluation, we <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\times T\times|C|\times R=10\times 6\times 11\times 15=9,900" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>9,900</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\times T\times|C|\times R=10\times 6\times 11\times 15=9,900</annotation></semantics></math> prompts per model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the results of evaluating nine LLMs on the full <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">MillStone</span> benchmark or (to reduce cost) the most controversial issues. We ran 15 trials for each of the six neutral prompts, to account for stochasticity in model responses.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Baseline stances</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by measuring the “inherent” stances, without arguments for either side of the issues.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11967v1/neither_all_models_gpt_grok_only.svg" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/454;" width="476" height="454"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Baseline stances on the issues where the models strongly disagree.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Walking on the sunny side of the street.</span> All models evaluated on the full <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">MillStone</span> show a marked bias towards the “pro” position on all issues (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.4 Quantifying open-mindedness ‣ 4 Measurement methodology ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).The share of “pro” responses exceeds that of “con” <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;50\%</annotation></semantics></math> for all models, reaching <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="78.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>78.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">78.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> for Llama 3.1 8B. For example, asked whether the US penny should stay in circulation, seven models overwhelmingly output that the penny should be taken out of circulation, whereas in 100% of trials Llama 3.1 8B outputs that the penny should stay.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline unanimity.</span> On 26 issues (<math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>), the evaluated LLMs are in near-unanimous agreement with each other (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.4 Quantifying open-mindedness ‣ 4 Measurement methodology ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>): the <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">maximum pairwise difference</span> (MPD) of baselines across models is less than <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math>. On 21 of these issues, the models are <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consistent</span>, i.e., nearly all of their responses support one side of the issue. The full list of these unanimous and consistent issues is given in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Unanimous and consistent issues ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline disagreements.</span>
On many issues, models disagree with each other. For example, 12 issues had MPDs over <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\%</annotation></semantics></math>. The full list of these issues, with the models that are most in support of each side, is in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Controversial issues ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.
For example, Llama 3.1 8B’s baseline stance is strongly in favor of the US military’s use of drone strikes and fighting in hockey, while Grok 3 mini is highly opposed. Unlike all other models, Claude Opus 4 is strongly against uniforms in schools, while Gemini 2.0 Flash opposes teacher tenure. Overall, Claude Opus 4 is often strongly against a position (e.g., moving Halloween to a Saturday, the economic benefits of holding the Olympics in a city, or positive assessment of Ronald Reagan’s presidency), while Llama 3.1 8B is often strongly in favor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We used the top ten issues on which the five models disagree for our evaluation of GPT 4o, GPT 4o mini, Grok 3, and Grok 3 mini (Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.1 Baseline stances ‣ 6 Results ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Opus 4 refuses to take a stance.</span> Claude Opus 4 is unique in frequently refusing to respond to a neutral prompt explicitly requesting that it take a stance. There are 19 issues (<math id="S6.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.8\%</annotation></semantics></math>) where <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;30\%</annotation></semantics></math> of its responses are refusals to answer with a position. This behavior is especially common on controversial political issues. The most-refused topics are gun control (<math id="S6.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> refusal rate), American socialism (<math id="S6.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="76.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>76.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">76.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> refusal rate), and reparations for slavery (<math id="S6.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="74.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>74.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">74.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> refusal rate). We hypothesize that Opus 4 has been intentionally trained or aligned to refuse to answer prompts about its political views.
<em id="S6.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">This behavior completely disappears when in-context arguments are added to the prompt</em>, even if the “pro” and “con” arguments are balanced.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Other models rarely refuse to pick a position when queried about their views on an issue.
Their average refusal rate across baselines is <math id="S6.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2\%</annotation></semantics></math>. See Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Opus won’t take sides ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> for the list of issues, refusal rates, and reversion to the mean in the presence of arguments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Stance shifts in response to arguments</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All evaluated models were relatively open-minded. Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Quantifying open-mindedness ‣ 4 Measurement methodology ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that their output stances shift in the direction of arguments, relative to the no-argument baseline. The shift is often more intense in the negative direction, which is likely explained by the baseline positive bias (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.4 Quantifying open-mindedness ‣ 4 Measurement methodology ‣ MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The notable outliers are GPT 4o and Grok 3 mini, which were evaluated only on the issues where the other models strongly disagree. On these issues, models had a negative baseline (40.0% and 30.0% “pro,” respectively), explaining their disproportionate shifts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
